      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-3-section-16",
        "title": "EKS Observability and Monitoring Integration",
        "content": "## EKS Observability and Monitoring Integration\n\n### Technical explanation\nAmazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer selects and operates workload capacity and configures the AWS resources surrounding the cluster. EKS architecture must be understood across the AWS account, VPC, cluster endpoint, IAM identity, worker compute, networking, storage, and application layers.\n\nThis section focuses on EKS Observability and Monitoring Integration. Explain Kubernetes metrics, container logs, control-plane logging, CloudWatch integration, Prometheus monitoring, and operational alerting. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\naws eks describe-cluster --name production-eks --query cluster.logging\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful command means the application is healthy. Correlate Kubernetes events, resource conditions, AWS resource state, and application logs. In a production environment, capture the output and timestamp before changing configuration so that the original failure evidence remains available.\n\n### Troubleshooting and failure analysis\nCommon failures include incorrect permissions, incompatible versions, missing network routes, exhausted resources, invalid configuration, unhealthy dependencies, and delayed reconciliation. Identify the failing layer first. Check whether the API request reached the cluster, whether the relevant object was accepted, whether the responsible controller or AWS integration acted, and whether the resulting workload or infrastructure became ready. Avoid deleting resources or changing multiple settings before collecting evidence.\n\n### CI/CD and production engineering\nA reliable delivery pipeline should validate manifests, verify image references, use narrowly scoped deployment identities, apply changes through controlled automation, and wait for rollout or resource readiness. Production teams should maintain version compatibility records, monitor resource capacity, protect secrets, restrict network exposure, and define rollback procedures. Changes to cluster infrastructure should be separated from application releases when their risk and recovery requirements differ.\n\n### Operational checklist\nConfirm ownership boundaries, permissions, dependencies, configuration, resource health, and monitoring. Record the expected state and compare it with the observed state. Verify that the relevant component is available, that its dependent services can communicate, and that failure signals are visible in logs or events. Document the corrective action and validate the application-level outcome before closing an incident."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-3-section-17",
        "title": "EKS Security Boundaries and Shared Responsibility",
        "content": "## EKS Security Boundaries and Shared Responsibility\n\n### Technical explanation\nAmazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer selects and operates workload capacity and configures the AWS resources surrounding the cluster. EKS architecture must be understood across the AWS account, VPC, cluster endpoint, IAM identity, worker compute, networking, storage, and application layers.\n\nThis section focuses on EKS Security Boundaries and Shared Responsibility. Describe IAM roles, security groups, network policies, secrets handling, encryption, image security, and the division of responsibilities between AWS and the customer. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\naws eks describe-cluster --name production-eks --query cluster.encryptionConfig\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful command means the application is healthy. Correlate Kubernetes events, resource conditions, AWS resource state, and application logs. In a production environment, capture the output and timestamp before changing configuration so that the original failure evidence remains available.\n\n### Troubleshooting and failure analysis\nCommon failures include incorrect permissions, incompatible versions, missing network routes, exhausted resources, invalid configuration, unhealthy dependencies, and delayed reconciliation. Identify the failing layer first. Check whether the API request reached the cluster, whether the relevant object was accepted, whether the responsible controller or AWS integration acted, and whether the resulting workload or infrastructure became ready. Avoid deleting resources or changing multiple settings before collecting evidence.\n\n### CI/CD and production engineering\nA reliable delivery pipeline should validate manifests, verify image references, use narrowly scoped deployment identities, apply changes through controlled automation, and wait for rollout or resource readiness. Production teams should maintain version compatibility records, monitor resource capacity, protect secrets, restrict network exposure, and define rollback procedures. Changes to cluster infrastructure should be separated from application releases when their risk and recovery requirements differ.\n\n### Operational checklist\nConfirm ownership boundaries, permissions, dependencies, configuration, resource health, and monitoring. Record the expected state and compare it with the observed state. Verify that the relevant component is available, that its dependent services can communicate, and that failure signals are visible in logs or events. Document the corrective action and validate the application-level outcome before closing an incident."
      },
