      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-4-section-18",
        "title": "Control Plane Troubleshooting and Production Validation",
        "content": "## Control Plane Troubleshooting and Production Validation\n\n### Technical explanation\nThe Kubernetes control plane is responsible for accepting API requests, storing cluster state, making scheduling decisions, and reconciling resources toward their declared configuration. In Amazon EKS, AWS manages the underlying control-plane infrastructure, but platform engineers remain responsible for Kubernetes objects, access design, workload configuration, and operational diagnosis.\n\nThis section focuses on Control Plane Troubleshooting and Production Validation. Build a diagnostic workflow for API availability, etcd-related symptoms, scheduling delays, controller reconciliation failures, and EKS-managed control-plane logging. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\nkubectl get --raw /readyz?verbose\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful command means the application is healthy. Correlate Kubernetes events, resource conditions, AWS resource state, and application logs. In a production environment, capture the output and timestamp before changing configuration so that the original failure evidence remains available.\n\n### Troubleshooting and failure analysis\nCommon failures include incorrect permissions, incompatible versions, missing network routes, exhausted resources, invalid configuration, unhealthy dependencies, and delayed reconciliation. Identify the failing layer first. Check whether the API request reached the cluster, whether the relevant object was accepted, whether the responsible controller or AWS integration acted, and whether the resulting workload or infrastructure became ready. Avoid deleting resources or changing multiple settings before collecting evidence.\n\n### CI/CD and production engineering\nA reliable delivery pipeline should validate manifests, verify image references, use narrowly scoped deployment identities, apply changes through controlled automation, and wait for rollout or resource readiness. Production teams should maintain version compatibility records, monitor resource capacity, protect secrets, restrict network exposure, and define rollback procedures. Changes to cluster infrastructure should be separated from application releases when their risk and recovery requirements differ.\n\n### Operational checklist\nConfirm ownership boundaries, permissions, dependencies, configuration, resource health, and monitoring. Record the expected state and compare it with the observed state. Verify that the relevant component is available, that its dependent services can communicate, and that failure signals are visible in logs or events. Document the corrective action and validate the application-level outcome before closing an incident."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Worker Nodes",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-5-section-1",
        "title": "Kubernetes Worker Node Architecture",
        "content": "## Kubernetes Worker Node Architecture\n\n### 1. Technical foundation\nKubernetes worker nodes provide the compute environment where application containers execute. In Amazon EKS, worker capacity can be supplied through managed node groups, self-managed EC2 instances, or Fargate for supported workloads. Node health directly affects scheduling, application availability, resource utilization, and recovery.\n\nThis section explains Kubernetes Worker Node Architecture. Explain the worker node as the execution environment for application Pods. Cover the operating system, kubelet, container runtime, networking components, storage integration, and the relationship with the Kubernetes control plane. These concepts are important because Kubernetes depends on clearly defined responsibilities between control-plane services, node-level agents, AWS integrations, and application workloads. A production engineer must understand not only what the component does, but also which resources it manages, which dependencies it requires, and how its health can be verified.\n\n### 2. Internal operation and lifecycle\nThe operational lifecycle begins with a declared Kubernetes resource or an infrastructure configuration change. Relevant Kubernetes components observe the change, validate the required configuration, and perform their responsibilities. The resulting state is reflected through API objects, resource conditions, events, metrics, and logs. In an EKS environment, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources interact across the cluster boundary. Correct diagnosis requires identifying which layer owns the failing operation.\n\n### 3. Practical implementation and commands\nStart investigation with this command:\n\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\n\nUse the correct cluster context, AWS region, namespace, and identity. Inspect the complete command output and compare it with the expected state. A command that exits successfully does not necessarily prove application health. For example, a Node object may exist while its kubelet is unhealthy, and an API endpoint may respond while a particular admission webhook or dependent service is failing.\n\n### 4. Production troubleshooting\nInvestigate failures layer by layer. Check connectivity, identity permissions, component readiness, resource conditions, recent events, and dependency health. For worker-related problems, inspect node conditions, resource pressure, runtime status, and Pod placement. For API and state-related problems, inspect API readiness, request failures, authorization decisions, audit information, and available EKS control-plane logs. Preserve diagnostic evidence before applying disruptive changes.\n\n### 5. Security and reliability practices\nApply least-privilege access, protect credentials, use supported software versions, and monitor resource and dependency health. Avoid granting broad permissions merely to bypass a failed request. Use controlled maintenance procedures, tested recovery plans, and documented ownership boundaries. In managed EKS environments, distinguish customer-configurable settings from infrastructure that AWS operates.\n\n### 6. CI/CD and operational validation\nA production delivery pipeline should validate configuration before applying changes, use dedicated deployment identities, capture deployment results, and verify the resulting Kubernetes state. Where node or control-plane dependencies are involved, ensure the platform has sufficient capacity and healthy dependencies before releasing workloads. Record the observed state, corrective action, and post-change verification so that future incidents can be diagnosed consistently.\n\n### 7. Practical engineering checklist\nConfirm the expected configuration, identify the responsible component, inspect its current status, verify permissions and dependencies, review events and logs, and compare actual behavior with the intended result. Apply the smallest safe correction, wait for reconciliation, and verify the final application outcome. Document unresolved risks and escalate infrastructure-level issues to the appropriate AWS or platform operations team."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-5-section-2",
        "title": "Worker Node Registration and Cluster Joining",
        "content": "## Worker Node Registration and Cluster Joining\n\n### 1. Technical foundation\nKubernetes worker nodes provide the compute environment where application containers execute. In Amazon EKS, worker capacity can be supplied through managed node groups, self-managed EC2 instances, or Fargate for supported workloads. Node health directly affects scheduling, application availability, resource utilization, and recovery.\n\nThis section explains Worker Node Registration and Cluster Joining. Describe how a new worker node receives cluster configuration, authenticates with the API server, registers its Node object, reports capacity, and becomes eligible for scheduling. These concepts are important because Kubernetes depends on clearly defined responsibilities between control-plane services, node-level agents, AWS integrations, and application workloads. A production engineer must understand not only what the component does, but also which resources it manages, which dependencies it requires, and how its health can be verified.\n\n### 2. Internal operation and lifecycle\nThe operational lifecycle begins with a declared Kubernetes resource or an infrastructure configuration change. Relevant Kubernetes components observe the change, validate the required configuration, and perform their responsibilities. The resulting state is reflected through API objects, resource conditions, events, metrics, and logs. In an EKS environment, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources interact across the cluster boundary. Correct diagnosis requires identifying which layer owns the failing operation.\n\n### 3. Practical implementation and commands\nStart investigation with this command:\n\nkubectl describe node <node-name>\n\nUse the correct cluster context, AWS region, namespace, and identity. Inspect the complete command output and compare it with the expected state. A command that exits successfully does not necessarily prove application health. For example, a Node object may exist while its kubelet is unhealthy, and an API endpoint may respond while a particular admission webhook or dependent service is failing.\n\n### 4. Production troubleshooting\nInvestigate failures layer by layer. Check connectivity, identity permissions, component readiness, resource conditions, recent events, and dependency health. For worker-related problems, inspect node conditions, resource pressure, runtime status, and Pod placement. For API and state-related problems, inspect API readiness, request failures, authorization decisions, audit information, and available EKS control-plane logs. Preserve diagnostic evidence before applying disruptive changes.\n\n### 5. Security and reliability practices\nApply least-privilege access, protect credentials, use supported software versions, and monitor resource and dependency health. Avoid granting broad permissions merely to bypass a failed request. Use controlled maintenance procedures, tested recovery plans, and documented ownership boundaries. In managed EKS environments, distinguish customer-configurable settings from infrastructure that AWS operates.\n\n### 6. CI/CD and operational validation\nA production delivery pipeline should validate configuration before applying changes, use dedicated deployment identities, capture deployment results, and verify the resulting Kubernetes state. Where node or control-plane dependencies are involved, ensure the platform has sufficient capacity and healthy dependencies before releasing workloads. Record the observed state, corrective action, and post-change verification so that future incidents can be diagnosed consistently.\n\n### 7. Practical engineering checklist\nConfirm the expected configuration, identify the responsible component, inspect its current status, verify permissions and dependencies, review events and logs, and compare actual behavior with the intended result. Apply the smallest safe correction, wait for reconciliation, and verify the final application outcome. Document unresolved risks and escalate infrastructure-level issues to the appropriate AWS or platform operations team."
      },
