        "id": "stage-10-topic-5-section-8",
        "title": "Node Labels, Selectors, and Workload Placement",
        "content": "## Node Labels, Selectors, and Workload Placement\n\n### 1. Technical foundation\nKubernetes worker nodes provide the compute environment where application containers execute. In Amazon EKS, worker capacity can be supplied through managed node groups, self-managed EC2 instances, or Fargate for supported workloads. Node health directly affects scheduling, application availability, resource utilization, and recovery.\n\nThis section explains Node Labels, Selectors, and Workload Placement. Explain node labels and how nodeSelector, node affinity, and required or preferred placement rules influence scheduling and workload isolation. These concepts are important because Kubernetes depends on clearly defined responsibilities between control-plane services, node-level agents, AWS integrations, and application workloads. A production engineer must understand not only what the component does, but also which resources it manages, which dependencies it requires, and how its health can be verified.\n\n### 2. Internal operation and lifecycle\nThe operational lifecycle begins with a declared Kubernetes resource or an infrastructure configuration change. Relevant Kubernetes components observe the change, validate the required configuration, and perform their responsibilities. The resulting state is reflected through API objects, resource conditions, events, metrics, and logs. In an EKS environment, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources interact across the cluster boundary. Correct diagnosis requires identifying which layer owns the failing operation.\n\n### 3. Practical implementation and commands\nStart investigation with this command:\n\nkubectl get nodes --show-labels\n\nUse the correct cluster context, AWS region, namespace, and identity. Inspect the complete command output and compare it with the expected state. A command that exits successfully does not necessarily prove application health. For example, a Node object may exist while its