        "id": "stage-10-topic-6-section-15",
        "title": "API Server Performance and Request Throttling",
        "content": "## API Server Performance and Request Throttling\n\n### 1. Technical foundation\nThe Kubernetes API server exposes the cluster API, while etcd provides persistent storage for Kubernetes cluster state. In Amazon EKS, AWS operates the managed control-plane infrastructure, so customers should use supported Kubernetes APIs, EKS configuration, and control-plane logging rather than assuming direct access to managed etcd hosts.\n\nThis section explains API Server Performance and Request Throttling. Explain API request volume, client-side throttling, API Priority and Fairness, expensive list operations, watch pressure, and techniques for reducing unnecessary API load. These concepts are important because Kubernetes depends on clearly defined responsibilities between control-plane services, node-level agents, AWS integrations, and application workloads. A production engineer must understand not only what the component does, but also which resources it manages, which dependencies it requires, and how its health can be verified.\n\n### 2. Internal operation and lifecycle\nThe operational lifecycle begins with a declared Kubernetes resource or an infrastructure configuration change. Relevant Kubernetes components observe the change, validate the required configuration, and perform their responsibilities. The resulting state is reflected through API objects, resource conditions, events, metrics, and logs. In an EKS environment, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources interact across the cluster boundary. Correct diagnosis requires identifying which layer owns the failing operation.\n\n### 3. Practical implementation and commands\nStart investigation with this command:\n\nkubectl get --raw /metrics\n\nUse the correct cluster context, AWS region, namespace, and identity. Inspect the complete command output and compare it with the expected state. A command that exits successfully does not necessarily prove application health. For example, a Node object may exist while its kubelet is unhealthy, and an API endpoint may respond while a particular admission webhook or dependent service is failing.\n\n### 4. Production troubleshooting\nInvestigate failures layer by layer. Check connectivity, identity permissions, component readiness, resource conditions, recent events, and dependency health. For worker-related problems, inspect node conditions, resource pressure, runtime status, and Pod placement. For API and state-related problems, inspect API readiness, request failures, authorization decisions, audit information, and available EKS control-plane logs. Preserve diagnostic evidence before applying disruptive changes.\n\n### 5. Security and reliability practices\nApply least-privilege access, protect credentials, use supported software versions, and monitor resource and dependency health. Avoid granting broad permissions merely to bypass a failed request. Use controlled maintenance procedures, tested recovery plans, and documented ownership boundaries. In managed EKS environments, distinguish customer-configurable settings from infrastructure that AWS operates.\n\n### 6. CI/CD and operational validation\nA production delivery pipeline should validate configuration before applying changes, use dedicated deployment identities, capture deployment results, and verify the resulting Kubernetes state. Where node or control-plane dependencies are involved, ensure the platform has sufficient capacity and healthy dependencies before releasing workloads. Record the observed state, corrective action, and post-change verification so that future incidents can be diagnosed consistently.\n\n### 7. Practical engineering checklist\nConfirm the expected configuration, identify the responsible component, inspect its current status, verify permissions and dependencies, review events and logs, and compare actual behavior with the intended result. Apply the smallest safe correction, wait for reconciliation, and verify the final application outcome. Document unresolved risks and escalate infrastructure-level issues to the appropriate AWS or platform operations team."
