      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-8-section-3",
        "title": "Single-Container and Multi-Container Pods",
        "content": "## Single-Container and Multi-Container Pods\n\n### 1. Technical foundation\nA Kubernetes Pod represents one or more tightly coupled containers that share selected execution resources and are scheduled as a unit. Pods are generally disposable: controllers create replacements when required, and a replacement Pod may receive a different name, UID, node, and IP address. Applications should therefore use stable Services and persistent storage where appropriate rather than depending on an individual Pod identity.\n\nThis section explains Single-Container and Multi-Container Pods. Compare single-container Pods with multi-container patterns such as sidecars, log collectors, proxies, and helper containers. Explain when shared lifecycle and localhost communication are useful. Understanding this behavior helps engineers distinguish configuration errors from scheduling decisions, node execution problems, and application-level failures. Kubernetes continuously processes desired state, but different components own different stages of that process. A reliable investigation identifies the responsible component before changing resources or restarting workloads.\n\n### 2. Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical lifecycle begins when a client submits a resource through the Kubernetes API. The API server validates and stores the object. Controllers may create or update dependent resources, and the scheduler makes placement decisions where scheduling is required. After assignment, the kubelet coordinates local execution through the container runtime and reports observed state. Networking, storage, security policies, and health checks contribute to the final workload outcome. A successful earlier stage does not prove that every later stage succeeded.\n\n### 3. Practical implementation and verification\nStart with this command:\n\nkubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n production\n\nUse the correct kubeconfig context and namespace. Inspect the full output, events, conditions, and related objects. For a scheduling or node execution issue, compare the Pod specification with node capacity and node conditions. For a Pod lifecycle issue, inspect container state, restart count, image reference, probe configuration, mounted volumes, and recent logs. Verify the observed behavior instead of relying only on a successful apply operation.\n\n### 4. Production troubleshooting\nCommon failure causes include insufficient resources, restrictive placement rules, unavailable images, invalid configuration, runtime errors, failed health probes, missing permissions, network restrictions, and storage attachment problems. Collect evidence using describe output, events, container logs, node status, and relevant AWS integration status. Change one cause at a time and verify the resulting state. Avoid deleting resources before understanding whether doing so could remove useful evidence or interrupt dependent workloads.\n\n### 5. Security and reliability engineering\nUse dedicated service accounts, least-privilege RBAC, controlled image sources, resource requests and limits, appropriate security contexts, and protected configuration. Design workloads to tolerate Pod replacement and node maintenance. Use readiness checks to control traffic eligibility, graceful termination to protect in-flight requests, and persistent storage for data that must survive Pod recreation. Do not treat a running container as proof that the application is ready or secure.\n\n### 6. CI/CD and operational validation\nA production pipeline should validate manifests, scan images, apply configuration through an approved identity, and wait for the required rollout or workload conditions. Capture deployment events and logs when validation fails. Confirm that the intended number of replicas becomes available, that application endpoints respond correctly, and that resource utilization remains within operational expectations. Keep rollback procedures and previous known-good configurations available.\n\n### 7. Practical engineering checklist\nConfirm the cluster context, namespace, resource specification, responsible controller, scheduling result, node health, runtime state, network connectivity, storage, security configuration, and application readiness. Compare expected and observed conditions, collect diagnostic evidence, apply the smallest safe fix, and verify the final user-facing behavior. Record the cause and preventive action so the same incident can be diagnosed faster in the future."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-8-section-4",
        "title": "Pod Creation and Admission Lifecycle",
        "content": "## Pod Creation and Admission Lifecycle\n\n### 1. Technical foundation\nA Kubernetes Pod represents one or more tightly coupled containers that share selected execution resources and are scheduled as a unit. Pods are generally disposable: controllers create replacements when required, and a replacement Pod may receive a different name, UID, node, and IP address. Applications should therefore use stable Services and persistent storage where appropriate rather than depending on an individual Pod identity.\n\nThis section explains Pod Creation and Admission Lifecycle. Trace Pod creation from manifest submission through API authentication, authorization, admission, persistence, scheduling, node assignment, and kubelet-driven container startup. Understanding this behavior helps engineers distinguish configuration errors from scheduling decisions, node execution problems, and application-level failures. Kubernetes continuously processes desired state, but different components own different stages of that process. A reliable investigation identifies the responsible component before changing resources or restarting workloads.\n\n### 2. Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical lifecycle begins when a client submits a resource through the Kubernetes API. The API server validates and stores the object. Controllers may create or update dependent resources, and the scheduler makes placement decisions where scheduling is required. After assignment, the kubelet coordinates local execution through the container runtime and reports observed state. Networking, storage, security policies, and health checks contribute to the final workload outcome. A successful earlier stage does not prove that every later stage succeeded.\n\n### 3. Practical implementation and verification\nStart with this command:\n\nkubectl apply --dry-run=server -f pod.yaml\n\nUse the correct kubeconfig context and namespace. Inspect the full output, events, conditions, and related objects. For a scheduling or node execution issue, compare the Pod specification with node capacity and node conditions. For a Pod lifecycle issue, inspect container state, restart count, image reference, probe configuration, mounted volumes, and recent logs. Verify the observed behavior instead of relying only on a successful apply operation.\n\n### 4. Production troubleshooting\nCommon failure causes include insufficient resources, restrictive placement rules, unavailable images, invalid configuration, runtime errors, failed health probes, missing permissions, network restrictions, and storage attachment problems. Collect evidence using describe output, events, container logs, node status, and relevant AWS integration status. Change one cause at a time and verify the resulting state. Avoid deleting resources before understanding whether doing so could remove useful evidence or interrupt dependent workloads.\n\n### 5. Security and reliability engineering\nUse dedicated service accounts, least-privilege RBAC, controlled image sources, resource requests and limits, appropriate security contexts, and protected configuration. Design workloads to tolerate Pod replacement and node maintenance. Use readiness checks to control traffic eligibility, graceful termination to protect in-flight requests, and persistent storage for data that must survive Pod recreation. Do not treat a running container as proof that the application is ready or secure.\n\n### 6. CI/CD and operational validation\nA production pipeline should validate manifests, scan images, apply configuration through an approved identity, and wait for the required rollout or workload conditions. Capture deployment events and logs when validation fails. Confirm that the intended number of replicas becomes available, that application endpoints respond correctly, and that resource utilization remains within operational expectations. Keep rollback procedures and previous known-good configurations available.\n\n### 7. Practical engineering checklist\nConfirm the cluster context, namespace, resource specification, responsible controller, scheduling result, node health, runtime state, network connectivity, storage, security configuration, and application readiness. Compare expected and observed conditions, collect diagnostic evidence, apply the smallest safe fix, and verify the final user-facing behavior. Record the cause and preventive action so the same incident can be diagnosed faster in the future."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-8-section-5",
        "title": "Pod Phases and Lifecycle States",
        "content": "## Pod Phases and Lifecycle States\n\n### 1. Technical foundation\nA Kubernetes Pod represents one or more tightly coupled containers that share selected execution resources and are scheduled as a unit. Pods are generally disposable: controllers create replacements when required, and a replacement Pod may receive a different name, UID, node, and IP address. Applications should therefore use stable Services and persistent storage where appropriate rather than depending on an individual Pod identity.\n\nThis section explains Pod Phases and Lifecycle States. Explain Pending, Running, Succeeded, Failed, and Unknown Pod phases, and distinguish the overall Pod phase from individual container states and readiness conditions. Understanding this behavior helps engineers distinguish configuration errors from scheduling decisions, node execution problems, and application-level failures. Kubernetes continuously processes desired state, but different components own different stages of that process. A reliable investigation identifies the responsible component before changing resources or restarting workloads.\n\n### 2. Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical lifecycle begins when a client submits a resource through the Kubernetes API. The API server validates and stores the object. Controllers may create or update dependent resources, and the scheduler makes placement decisions where scheduling is required. After assignment, the kubelet coordinates local execution through the container runtime and reports observed state. Networking, storage, security policies, and health checks contribute to the final workload outcome. A successful earlier stage does not prove that every later stage succeeded.\n\n### 3. Practical implementation and verification\nStart with this command:\n\nkubectl get pods -n production\n\nUse the correct kubeconfig context and namespace. Inspect the full output, events, conditions, and related objects. For a scheduling or node execution issue, compare the Pod specification with node capacity and node conditions. For a Pod lifecycle issue, inspect container state, restart count, image reference, probe configuration, mounted volumes, and recent logs. Verify the observed behavior instead of relying only on a successful apply operation.\n\n### 4. Production troubleshooting\nCommon failure causes include insufficient resources, restrictive placement rules, unavailable images, invalid configuration, runtime errors, failed health probes, missing permissions, network restrictions, and storage attachment problems. Collect evidence using describe output, events, container logs, node status, and relevant AWS integration status. Change one cause at a time and verify the resulting state. Avoid deleting resources before understanding whether doing so could remove useful evidence or interrupt dependent workloads.\n\n### 5. Security and reliability engineering\nUse dedicated service accounts, least-privilege RBAC, controlled image sources, resource requests and limits, appropriate security contexts, and protected configuration. Design workloads to tolerate Pod replacement and node maintenance. Use readiness checks to control traffic eligibility, graceful termination to protect in-flight requests, and persistent storage for data that must survive Pod recreation. Do not treat a running container as proof that the application is ready or secure.\n\n### 6. CI/CD and operational validation\nA production pipeline should validate manifests, scan images, apply configuration through an approved identity, and wait for the required rollout or workload conditions. Capture deployment events and logs when validation fails. Confirm that the intended number of replicas becomes available, that application endpoints respond correctly, and that resource utilization remains within operational expectations. Keep rollback procedures and previous known-good configurations available.\n\n### 7. Practical engineering checklist\nConfirm the cluster context, namespace, resource specification, responsible controller, scheduling result, node health, runtime state, network connectivity, storage, security configuration, and application readiness. Compare expected and observed conditions, collect diagnostic evidence, apply the smallest safe fix, and verify the final user-facing behavior. Record the cause and preventive action so the same incident can be diagnosed faster in the future."
